plan Statecharts
title   Statechart authoring and ordered turns
status  archived
goal
    Let an author describe one complete static actor family and reusable behavior with exact
    inference, deterministic state paths, and indivisible accepted turns. This proposed Plan owns
    definition and machine APIs, their types, state semantics, timers, and continuing declarations.
    Actor admission, context propagation and publication consumers use
    @plan.ApplicationsAndActors.O4, @plan.ApplicationsAndActors.O6 and
    @plan.ApplicationsAndActors.O7. No statement here claims implementation or approval.
outcomes
    O1  Definitions produce exact inert nominal values
        definition(config) synchronously returns the direct Effect Result of a Definition or
        canonical Diagnostic. It owns explicit id, recursive states, events, optional provider
        context selectors, the sole memory initializer and a flat named operation catalogue.
        The configuration uses these exact fields:

        | Field | Accepted value |
        | --- | --- |
        | id | Exact authored durable name |
        | states | Ordered recursive leaf/compound declarations |
        | events | Named record of "bare" or callable event declarations |
        | context? | Named record of provider Definition.select(selector) values |
        | operations? | Flat named resource, transaction and stream descriptor record |
        | memory? | Pure input-to-memory factory |

        Provider.select(selector) is an inert Definition-level authoring call. Its selector reads
        the provider's exact snapshot and preserves the selected result type. For example,
        context: { sessionState: Session.select(({ state }) => state) } declares one provider edge.
        A memory projection may use Theme.select(({ memory }) => memory.mode). The binding supplies
        one exact compatible provider ref under @plan.ApplicationsAndActors.O6; the selected value
        is not that ref. Declaring the edge creates no actor, subscription or work.

        operations: { routeConfig, submitIntent, submissionProgress } binds those exact descriptors
        to the same named O keys. The record is flat and closed. It adds no generic registry or
        alternative operation container. The three names illustrate resource/transaction/stream
        descriptors; their family contracts remain with their owning Plans.

        Supporting [API examples](references/README.md) remain uncompiled and unrun.

        Authored IDs and local state/event names retain exact spelling. Each is nonempty and at most
        256 UTF-8 bytes. Reject C0 controls, DEL, NUL and lone surrogates before constructing
        nominal tokens or durable identity.

        Exact composed and decomposed spellings remain distinct. Use no Unicode normalization or
        locale comparison. Composite identity uses a fixed namespace tag and length-prefixed UTF-8
        segments, never delimiter concatenation. Stable actor refs use the exact wire form owned by
        @plan.ApplicationsAndActors.O4. Opaque refs are runtime-local with no durable form.

        Shared portability rules belong to @plan.PackageContract.C1.

        Decode the ordinary authored shape once through one package-private Effect Schema before
        constructing nominal definition, state, event, envelope and context-selector values. Authors
        supply callable event declarations or the exact string "bare". They import no Schema and use
        no validatedEvent helper.

        Event constructors decode payloads on each call, returning direct Result<EventEnvelope,
        Diagnostic>. Bare constructors have zero arguments. Event payloads cannot be arrays,
        custom-prototype records, or contain an authored type field.

        Reject duplicate or partial declarations, invalid kinds and extra top-level fields.

        Literal IDs, state paths, event names, parameter tuples, payloads, selected context and 
        named
        families remain exact without as const, satisfies, explicit generics or wrappers. Event
        tokens are nominal; structural lookalikes are not assignable. EventOf extracts successful
        envelopes rather than Result wrappers. StateOf and EventOf accept Definition or Machine.

        memory: ({ input }) => Memory is the sole InputOf/MemoryOf source. Omitting its argument
        fixes Input to void; omitting memory yields void input and canonical empty memory. A fresh
        actor calls the pure initializer exactly once.

        Restore/resume never replay it or input. Later behavior sees memory, context, state and
        events, never original input. Only accepted transitions change actor-local memory.

        Memory is not cache, projection or operation status. Context identifying another domain
        instance requires host replacement/re-keying, not memory reuse under a changed domain
        identity.
    O2  Machines check one closed state and event universe
        machine(definitionResult, callback) matches the Result first. On failure it propagates the
        identical failure without invoking the callback. On success it supplies exact S, E, O,
        onContext, onMemory, invalidate and clear, and returns the direct machine Result.

        Behavior cannot enlarge the Definition universe. Type inference proceeds from definition to
        behavior in one direction, using NoInfer or equivalent constraints.

        State declarations are ordered recursive string leaves or single-key compounds, at most ten
        levels deep. Root and every compound require an exact states record and direct-child
        default. Leaves are explicit; {} is an empty leaf.

        Missing, extra, misplaced, duplicate and non-direct nodes reject. Complete paths such as
        S.ACTIVE.S.EDITING are nominal tokens. Publish exactly one active leaf; matches accepts that
        leaf or its active ancestor.

        Only on, redirect, activities and timers are node behavior fields. Compounds also contain
        default/states. The exact forms are:

        | Boundary | Accepted form |
        | --- | --- |
        | Event handler | Exact state-token shorthand, one transition, or ordered transitions |
        | Transition | target; optional guard, updateMemory, actions and exact-token reenter |
        | Redirect | One redirect or ordered entries with when and required target |
        | Timer | delay, guard, required explicit-event target, updateMemory |

        Defaults and redirects
        stabilize outermost-to-leaf, preserving authored order per node, without intermediate work
        or publication. Active compound redirects remain applicable after descendant transitions
        and memory changes. reenter names an exact active containing boundary; it releases that
        boundary's path and re-enters its target inside that boundary. Reject Boolean, foreign,
        inactive and non-containing reentry, relative string targets and runtime path lookup.

        Recursive substates share memory, context, event protocol, mailbox, operations and lifetime.
        They have no actor identity, input, address, output, completion or snapshot.
        Terminal-looking leaves remain ordinary leaves.

        They do not dispose actors, complete streams or close mailboxes. Independent actors may
        share a Machine; overlapping operation states do not become a Cartesian product of mutually
        exclusive machine modes.
    O3  Event macrosteps validate before indivisible publication
        An accepted event follows this order:

        1. Select the first authored eligible transition and evaluate its guard.
        2. Evaluate updateMemory, then actions against the same pre-turn snapshot and causal event.
        3. Apply target and memory to the candidate; stabilize redirects.
        4. Materialize and validate the complete finite-action batch; stage actor/store changes.
        5. Publish the committed cut, then start or join asynchronous work.

        A declined guard prevents that transition's later callbacks; later alternatives may still
        be considered. updateMemory returns Partial<Memory> and shallow-merges once. Actions cannot
        read candidate memory. Redirects read candidate state, memory and snapshot, never the event.

        No handler or all guards declining is a silent no-op: no issue, publication, TurnRecord,
        occurrence, work or consumed revision. Private Story acknowledgment still settles once.
        Root can(snapshot, event): boolean queries the captured snapshot's exact Machine. Its event
        is that Machine's decoded EventOf envelope. The snapshot and event cannot infer a widened
        or cross-machine universe. This retains the standalone two-argument form; it accepts neither
        a Machine in place of the snapshot nor an event-constructor Result.

        Bound can(event) in passive selection applies the same query to that selected snapshot cut.
        Both forms return true exactly when an authored transition is eligible; no handler or all
        guards declining returns false. They follow the same ordered eligibility/guard rules.
        They do not mutate, admit commands, publish issues or stabilize redirects.
        They materialize no actions, acquire no operations and perform no work.
        Command admission and lifecycle are separate questions.
        These typed calls require genuine compatible snapshots and decoded events. No raw/untrusted
        JavaScript decoder or new misuse-error promise is added.

        Finite actions originate only from accepted event transitions. A batch is one inert plan, a
        finite list, null, an empty list, or omission. Invalid member/boundary/target/key rejects
        the entire batch; no valid prefix escapes.

        Reject microstep 101 and ambiguous same-event ancestor/descendant handlers. User Effects
        never run during planning or before publication. Projection-only facts publish complete
        current projections without transition, guard, action, redirect, memory-update, finite-work
        or hidden-event evaluation.

        Explicit mapped events become later ordinary mailbox turns.

        A pre-commit authored callback defect preserves prior state, memory, bindings and store
        truth, publishes one issue-only snapshot, records ordered stable diagnostics, starts no
        occurrence/work and does not retry. The rule includes guard, memory, action, redirect, key,
        target, timer, binding and selector callbacks. After commit the cut cannot be abandoned or
        described as rollback. Atomic publication, acknowledgment, fatal invariant closure and
        failure projection follow @plan.ApplicationsAndActors.O7 and
        @plan.DiagnosticsContract.O2.
    O4  Continuing declarations and timers have exact lifetimes
        State activities and independent onMemory/onMemory.select declarations own continuing
        resource/stream plans. Each declaration returns one plan, false or null, never a
        runtime-sized list. A sentinel releases only that slot.

        Identity is compiled declaration slot, operation kind, descriptor and canonical K; equal
        normalized identity retains its generation and original executable P. Changed identity
        releases old ownership exactly once. Compound activities start parent-to-child and stop
        child-to-parent.

        Descendant changes do not restart unchanged parent work.

        onMemory.select uses whole-value Object.is for scalar/non-record results and fixed-key
        fieldwise Object.is for non-null plain records. It accepts no comparator. Activation/reentry
        receives current and previous undefined; later selected changes receive the preceding value.

        Definition-level context selectors are graph edges; onContext.select is a machine-level
        registration. The initial selection is silent. Later selected changes receive current and
        previous and return one exact self-event, false or null.

        Only that ordinary event handler evaluates transition legality and finite actions.
        @plan.ApplicationsAndActors.O6 owns causal context completeness and failure recovery.

        Timer names are unique machine-wide and get(name) accepts only the inferred name union.
        TimerSnapshot is the following closed inferred union; it adds no public root alias:

        | status | Fields present |
        | --- | --- |
        | idle | name |
        | scheduled | name, state, generation, startedAt, dueAt |
        | fired | name, state, generation, startedAt, dueAt, firedAt |
        | interrupt | name, state, generation, startedAt, dueAt |

        state is the exact active-leaf union; name is the machine-wide timer-name union. Missing
        fields remain absent. Timestamps use Runtime Clock integer epoch milliseconds.

        Delay normalization rejects invalid duration before mutation. Due timers consume one
        generation once and publish fired even when the timer guard declines. Timer callbacks
        receive state, memory, snapshot and timer { name, startedAt, dueAt }.

        Timers target explicit events, never finite actions. The timer turn and target event are
        separate FIFO macrosteps with their own pre-turn memory and optional update. Append the
        target after timer publication; queued work may intervene.

        Compound timers start on compound activation, retain absolute deadlines through descendant
        changes, cancel on compound exit, and restart only on exact owning-boundary reentry.
        Equal-deadline ordering is deterministic and Flow-owned, not host sleep race order.
        Existing after timers plus explicit refresh events provide polling: one due generation,
        next refresh only after settlement, failure waits one interval without implicit retry.
        Suspension/resume retains deadlines and performs at most one overdue refresh under
        @plan.ApplicationsAndActors.O5.
decisions
    D1  One Definition supplies shape and one Machine supplies behavior
        Keep the static family separate from reusable behavior. Definition construction is inert;
        machine consumes the validated result without importing the private Schema or re-decoding.
        The compiled configuration must preserve exact grammar and deterministic traversal rules.
        No runtime tree representation or specific table layout is a consumer contract.
    D2  One turn exposes one accepted cut
        Full-batch validation and post-publication work prevent partial candidate escape. Private
        plan shapes, scheduling primitives and reservation steps are private design choices.
        Observable purity, atomicity, ordering and single ownership remain binding.
constraints
    C1  Keep the authoring and statechart surface closed
        Exclude child-machine constructors/descriptors/inputs/completion/addresses, final kind,
        onDone/final output, context mutation, entry/exit/invoke/always, transition submit,
        anonymous Effect callbacks, registered views, dynamic state/operation registration and
        generic activity kits. Reject timer-owned finite actions and automatic operation retries.
    C2  Keep trust and type guarantees exact
        Caller-controlled authoring input is not an untrusted JavaScript reflection boundary. No
        stable rejection/translation promise covers proxies, throwing getters, descriptor tricks,
        symbols, sparse arrays, custom containers or concurrent mutation during construction.
        Validate ordinary malformed objects.

        Byte counts, surrogate checks and depth limits are synchronous runtime validation; do not
        encode UTF-8 arithmetic or recursive depth counters in public types. No any/assertion
        erasure may hide a type or behavior failure.
verification
    V1  Definition grammar, identity and literal inference
        Establish @O1 with positive and negative compile fixtures plus construction tests. Accept
        depth ten, exact literal/event/InputOf/MemoryOf unions and zero-argument bare events. Reject
        depth eleven, invalid names, 257-byte names, controls, lone surrogates, malformed payloads,
        structural event lookalikes, unknown fields, authored void input and alternate memory
        sources.

        ASCII and 2/3/4-byte vectors prove bytes, exact spellings and portable identity
        independently of TypeScript. Assert construction starts no actors, storage, services or
        Effects. Planned checks: rewrite check:types plus focused definition tests.

        Executable proof not yet established for this proposed contract. @V2 through @V4 define the
        remaining cases.

        Compile the exact context and operations fields above with state and memory provider
        projections. Preserve each provider result and named family. Reject alternate containers,
        wrong provider bindings and unknown declaration fields without widening the Definition.

        Candidate check: [definition 
        tests](../../../packages/flow-state-rewrite/src/definition/test/definition.test.ts).
        The complete cases above remain planned; this link is not a coverage or execution claim.
    V2  Exact configuration, paths and reentry
        Establish @O2 by compiling recursive positive/negative grammar cases and running defaults,
        compound redirects, leaf/ancestor matching and exact reentry. Missing/extra/non-direct 
        nodes,
        foreign/inactive/non-containing boundaries and ambiguous handlers reject before work.
        Verify one actor identity through nested state changes and ordinary terminal-looking leaves.
        Planned checks: rewrite machine owner tests and packed declarations; not executed here.

        Candidate check: [machine 
        tests](../../../packages/flow-state-rewrite/src/machine/test/machine.test.ts).
        The complete cases above remain planned; this link is not a coverage or execution claim.
    V3  Macrostep success, no-op and failure lanes
        Establish @O3 by asserting updateMemory runs before actions and both observe the same
        pre-turn snapshot and causal event. Actions cannot observe updated memory. Test declined
        alternatives, candidate redirect reads, one shallow merge and complete-batch failure with a
        valid prefix.
        Valid turns publish one cut before work. No-handler/all-decline settle
        privately with zero revision/evidence/work.

        Guard/action/redirect/key defects retain prior domain/store truth, publish exactly one
        issue-only cut and do not retry. Microstep 101 rejects without residue. Projection facts
        invoke none of the transition callbacks; mapped events run later.

        Compile root can(snapshot, decodedEvent) and bound can(decodedEvent) for one exact Machine.
        Reject wrong-machine events, Machine-as-snapshot and constructor Results at compile time.
        For valid snapshots, both query forms agree on true/false eligibility and leave revisions,
        issues, evidence, ownership and work unchanged. Querying an older snapshot uses its captured
        state, memory, context and passive reader cut rather than current live truth.

        Planned checks: production turn/action tests plus @plan.ApplicationsAndActors.V7; unbuilt.

        Candidate check: [turn 
        tests](../../../packages/flow-state-rewrite/src/runtime-next/test/turns.test.ts).
        The complete cases above remain planned; this link is not a coverage or execution claim.
    V4  Continuing identity, timer ordering and cancellation
        Establish @O4 by keeping an equal fresh plan's original P/generation, replacing unequal
        identity once, and releasing only one slot with false/null. Exercise parent/child lifetime,
        selector equality, current/previous types and cancellation/finalizer failure. Test timer
        name narrowing, equal deadlines, guard decline, descendant continuity and exact reentry.

        Queue an event between timer and target: each update reads its own FIFO pre-turn memory.
        Resume overdue timers once without replay; invalid delay or timer actions change nothing.
        Planned checks: production activity/timer tests and typed fixtures; not executed here.


        Candidate check: 
        [tests](../../../packages/flow-state-rewrite/src/runtime-next/test/actors.test.ts).
        The complete cases above remain planned; this link is not a coverage or execution claim.
